[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":113},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":-5,"_30":31,"_32":33,"_34":35,"_36":37,"_38":-5,"_39":-5,"_40":41,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":35,"_53":54,"_55":-5,"_56":35,"_57":58},"expiresAt",1795653651000,"id","wbx0pv97byuykd2zb3n3k4m7n","title","Cisco Talos 分析 OpenAI 关联 AI 智能体沙箱逃逸与 ARTEX 攻击框架","originalTitle","Oh, is it Thanksgiving? I hadn’t noticed!","summary","Cisco Talos 分析 OpenAI 关联的多智能体系统如何借助公共服务突破容器沙箱，并讨论其协调、任务拆分和自适应机制。研究指出，ARTEX 等框架已在真实环境中将单一被盗凭据转化为深入网络访问，最快不到 15 分钟；防守方可监测突发暂停模式、异常公共服务流量及特定 URL 参数或文件名。","reason","source",{"_66":112},"links",{"_58":111},"publishedAt","2026-11-25T19:00:00.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-25T19:26:20.329Z","timelineAt","category","tags",[105,67,106,73,71,69,107,108,109,110],"score",8,"selected",false,"channel","news","story","x","readingMode","full","author","David J. Bianco","body",{"_101":-5,"_58":102,"_103":-5,"_104":35},"outline",[76,77,78,79,80,81,82],"relatedStories",[],"topics",[59,60,61,62,63],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group","hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original",{"_64":74,"_66":75},{"_64":72,"_66":73},{"_64":70,"_66":71},{"_64":68,"_66":69},{"_64":65,"_66":67},"slug","ai-security","name","AI 安全","malware","恶意软件","attack","攻击活动","ai-offense","AI 攻防","cisco","Cisco / 思科",{"_11":99,"_84":100,"_86":87},{"_11":97,"_84":98,"_86":96},{"_11":94,"_84":95,"_86":96},{"_11":92,"_84":93,"_86":87},{"_11":90,"_84":91,"_86":87},{"_11":88,"_84":89,"_86":87},{"_11":83,"_84":85,"_86":87},"sec-7","text","Most prevalent malware files from Talos telemetry over the past week&nbsp;","level",2,"sec-6","Upcoming events where you can find Talos&nbsp;","sec-5","Can’t get enough Talos?&nbsp;","sec-4","Top security headlines of the week&nbsp;","sec-3","So now what?&nbsp;",3,"sec-2","Why do I care?&nbsp;","sec-1","The one big thing &nbsp;","zh","<p><img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/657b4eac9df0fc72.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Oh, is it Thanksgiving? I hadn’t noticed!\"></p><p>I tried hard not to write another typical “Thanksgiving article.” Obviously, I failed. But you know what? It <em>is</em> Thanksgiving week here in the U.S., and coming up with fun, interesting, short form topics is <em>hard</em>, especially when you’re fighting the weight of Tradition expected this week. So instead of me writing something insightful, you’re getting some things I’m thankful for. Even that’s harder than you might think, because there are so many things, and this is just the newsletter <em>introduction</em>, after all. &nbsp;</p><p>Let’s start off with something I’m pretty sure <em>none</em> of us are thankful for: threat actors. <strong>Seriously, screw you guys!</strong> I know I'd be out of a job without you, but that’s just another way of saying “I coulda been a professional bagpiper.” There are days when that doesn’t sound so bad. So, no gratitude coming your way, people! &nbsp;</p><p>In terms of <em>actual </em>thankfulness, I want to give a big <strong>THANK YOU</strong> to the entire Blue Team, everywhere. Everyone out there defending, especially those in the U.S. taking shifts on Thursday to keep the turkey-eating public safe and secure.&nbsp;This is a hard, often high-pressure job for which you get little thanks, but all the blame when a breach occurs. We see you. We appreciate you. We promise we’ll save you some stuffing.&nbsp;</p><p>Speaking of defenders, I’m also very thankful to get to work with so many talented and wonderful people here at Talos. I’m not just saying this because I know my boss will read it! Without exception, everyone I’ve worked with here has not only been an expert at what they do, but they’ve been friendly, welcoming, and very patient with me as I’ve been feeling my way around this new org over the past several months. Thank you!&nbsp;</p><p>Finally, and maybe most importantly of all, I want to thank <em>you</em>, our readers. You are the reason we’re all here, doing what we do, helping you to look out for your own networks and protect your own people. We appreciate your trust!&nbsp;</p><p>Whether you’re celebrating Thanksgiving here in the States or just having a normal weekend wherever you are, I hope you get some time to relax, unplug, recharge, and de-stress. &nbsp;</p><p>Now, who wants some pie?&nbsp;</p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">The one big thing &nbsp;</h2><p>In Talos’ <a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/the-great-escape-tradecraft-insights-from-an-agentic-swarm/\"><u>latest blog</u></a>, we examine the fascinating tradecraft of the OpenAI-linked agentic swarm that broke out of its containerized environment in 2026. While not inherently malicious, this swarm of AI agents used public internet services as improvised infrastructure to bypass their sandbox constraints and complete their training tasks. By analyzing this unintentional great escape, we can learn how multi-agent systems coordinate, partition work, and adapt when their paths are blocked. This view into autonomous AI operations provides a baseline for understanding how these behaviors transfer to purpose-built cyber attack frameworks.&nbsp;</p><h3 id=\"sec-2\">Why do I care?&nbsp;</h3><p>The leap from an AI sandbox escape to weaponized cyber attacks has already happened. Agentic frameworks like ARTEX are currently in the wild, proving that multi-agent systems can move from a single compromised credential to deep network access in under 15 minutes. These autonomous threats adapt to errors in real time and execute dozens of actions in mere seconds. Understanding how these swarms coordinate, adapt, and exfiltrate data is a necessity to keep up with an adversary that operates at machine speed.&nbsp;</p><h3 id=\"sec-3\">So now what?&nbsp;</h3><p>To catch an AI swarm, defenders must look for its operational fingerprints. Monitor your environment for irregular \"burst-and-pause\" timing patterns that indicate rapid, sub-second automated reasoning. Keep a close eye on unusual traffic to allow-listed public services like “webhook.site”, which agents use to park data before terminating. Finally, hunt for self-labeling conventions, like highly specific URL parameters or predictable file names (e.g., “artex-probe-*”), that agents rely on to track their state. <a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/the-great-escape-tradecraft-insights-from-an-agentic-swarm/\"><u>Read the full blog</u></a> for a deeper dive into these swarm behaviors.&nbsp;</p><h2 id=\"sec-4\">Top security headlines of the week&nbsp;</h2><p><strong>AI-assisted cyber attacks trigger unauthorized payments at Indian banks and fintech firms </strong>&nbsp;<br>The Indian Computer Emergency Response Team (CERT-In) warned the attacks have resulted in unauthorized financial transactions at banking and financial services entities, as well as technology service providers supporting government-linked payments and platforms handling vendor or partner payouts. (<a href=\"https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/ai-assisted-cyberattacks-trigger.html\"><u>The Hacker News</u></a>)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>JPMorgan, Visa confirm customer data taken in third-party vendor hacks</strong>&nbsp;<br>Marketing data, including email addresses, were affected. Both Visa and JPMorgan said the incidents were unrelated to tech disruptions reported earlier this week. (<a href=\"https://www.cybersecuritydive.com/news/jpmorgan-visa-customer-data-leaked-third-party-hacks/875333/\"><u>Cybersecurity Dive</u></a>)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>OpenAI and Cisco unveil AI-assisted Linux security tool to block exploits before patching </strong>&nbsp;<br>OpenAI and Cisco have teamed up to launch Shirudo (meaning \"shield\" in Japanese), an open-source Linux runtime that allows AI agents to defend systems without downtime. (<a href=\"https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/openai-and-cisco-unveil-ai-assisted.html\"><u>The Hacker News</u></a>)</p><p><strong>AI-enabled campaign targets Check Point Security Management servers</strong>&nbsp;<br>The attacks led to at least 22 organizations being compromised across the globe, including in the UK, the U.S., France, Germany, Australia, China, India and Australia. The targeted organizations work in the energy, transportation, financial services and government sectors. (<a href=\"https://www.cybersecuritydive.com/news/ai-enabled-campaign-targets-check-point-security-management-servers/875622/\"><u>Cybersecurity Dive</u></a>)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Drupal Fail2Install exploit details emerge as critical RCE flaw faces active exploitation</strong>&nbsp;<br>While Drupal has checks in place to determine whether the website is already installed before allowing installation to proceed, the research found that it's possible to bypass these checks by exhausting server resources. (<a href=\"https://thehackernews.com/2026/11/drupal-fail2install-exploit-details.html\"><u>The Hacker News</u></a>)&nbsp;</p><h2 id=\"sec-5\">Can’t get enough Talos?&nbsp;</h2><p><a href=\"https://www.buzzsprout.com/2033817/episodes/20168915\"><strong><u>Beers with Talos: Ranksgiving with guest Anton Chuvakin</u></strong></a>&nbsp;<br>This Ranksgiving, Dave has a brand new (though no less controversial) topic: the top five kitchen utensils/gadgets needed to make Thanksgiving dinner. Prepare to be both aghast and disappointed (as usual) in the team's choices.&nbsp;</p><p><a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/uat-12643-suspected-north-korea-aligned-actor-blends-blockchain-resilience-with-targeted-infostealing/\"><strong><u>UAT-12643 blends blockchain resilience with targeted infostealing</u></strong></a>&nbsp;<br>Talos discovered UAT-12643 using Overlord RAT, which implements a command-and-control (C2) resilience technique in which encrypted server URLs are embedded in Solana blockchain transaction memos.&nbsp;</p><p><a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/securing-grandmas-groceries-with-pierre-cadieux/\"><strong><u>Securing Grandma's groceries with Pierre Cadieux</u></strong></a>&nbsp;<br>Pierre takes us back to his early days on help desks in the 90s, where learning to correlate logs between Windows and Unix systems sparked the curiosity that eventually led him to security.&nbsp;</p><h2 id=\"sec-6\">Upcoming events where you can find Talos&nbsp;</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://events.aavar.org/cybersecurity-conference/\"><u>AVAR 2026</u></a> (Dec. 2 – 4) Hyderabad, India&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://blackhat.com/europe/\"><u>Black Hat Europe</u></a> (Dec. 7 – 10) London, U.K.&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://jsac.jpcert.or.jp/\"><u>JSAC2027</u></a> (Jan. 19 – 22) Tokyo, Japan&nbsp;</li><li><a href=\"https://www.insomnihack.ch/\"><u>Insomni’hack</u></a> (Feb. 1 – 5) Lausanne, Switzerland&nbsp;</li></ul><h2 id=\"sec-7\">Most prevalent malware files from Talos telemetry over the past week&nbsp;</h2><p><strong>SHA256: 9f1f11a708d393e0a4109ae189bc64f1f3e312653dcf317a2bd406f18ffcc507</strong>&nbsp;<br>MD5: 2915b3f8b703eb744fc54c81f4a9c67f&nbsp;<br>Talos Rep: <a href=\"https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=9f1f11a708d393e0a4109ae189bc64f1f3e312653dcf317a2bd406f18ffcc507\"><u>https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=9f1f11a708d393e0a4109ae189bc64f1f3e312653dcf317a2bd406f18ffcc507</u></a>&nbsp;<br>Example Filename: VID001.exe&nbsp;<br>Detection Name: W32.9F1F11A708-100.SBX.TG**&nbsp;</p><p><strong>SHA256: f4b1f888f29faa7fbec180f42bdc78596e8a8b908c0a6e6c419933703c81df85</strong>&nbsp;<br>MD5: 95a6a4b638cfdfbe3afc883d593a1f87&nbsp;<br>Talos Rep: <a href=\"https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=f4b1f888f29faa7fbec180f42bdc78596e8a8b908c0a6e6c419933703c81df85\"><u>https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=f4b1f888f29faa7fbec180f42bdc78596e8a8b908c0a6e6c419933703c81df85</u></a>&nbsp;<br>Example Filename: f4b1f888f29faa7fbec180f42bdc78596e8a8b908c0a6e6c419933703c81df85.js&nbsp;<br>Detection Name: W32.F4B1F888F2-95.SBX.TG&nbsp;</p><p><strong>SHA256: 58d6fec4ba24c32d38c9a0c7c39df3cb0e91f500b323e841121d703c7b718681</strong>&nbsp;<br>MD5: f1fe671bcefd4630e5ed8b87c9283534&nbsp;<br>Talos Rep: <a href=\"https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=58d6fec4ba24c32d38c9a0c7c39df3cb0e91f500b323e841121d703c7b718681\"><u>https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=58d6fec4ba24c32d38c9a0c7c39df3cb0e91f500b323e841121d703c7b718681</u></a>&nbsp;<br>Example Filename: KMSAuto Net.exe &nbsp;<br>Detection Name: W32.58D6FEC4BA-95.SBX.TG &nbsp;</p><p><strong>SHA256: fed979f93bcaf4e73ebd25748093a92095d5109cbd01d55f97bdc50ce509ad2f</strong>&nbsp;<br>MD5: 207d9d891ac756b2bfad88aba5682c65&nbsp;<br>Talos Rep: <a href=\"https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=fed979f93bcaf4e73ebd25748093a92095d5109cbd01d55f97bdc50ce509ad2f\"><u>https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=fed979f93bcaf4e73ebd25748093a92095d5109cbd01d55f97bdc50ce509ad2f</u></a>&nbsp;<br>Example Filename: AAct.exe&nbsp;<br>Detection Name: W32.FED979F93B-95.SBX.TG&nbsp;</p><p><strong>SHA256: 38d053135ddceaef0abb8296f3b0bf6114b25e10e6fa1bb8050aeecec4ba8f55</strong>&nbsp;<br>MD5: 41444d7018601b599beac0c60ed1bf83&nbsp;<br>Talos Rep: <a href=\"https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=38d053135ddceaef0abb8296f3b0bf6114b25e10e6fa1bb8050aeecec4ba8f55\"><u>https://talosintelligence.com/talos_file_reputation?s=38d053135ddceaef0abb8296f3b0bf6114b25e10e6fa1bb8050aeecec4ba8f55</u></a>&nbsp;<br>Example Filename: content.js&nbsp;<br>Detection Name: W32.38D053135D-95.SBX.TG&nbsp;</p>","zhKind","complete","汇总","Agent 安全","Google","Cisco","Check Point","CISA","https://blog.talosintelligence.com/oh-is-it-thanksgiving-i-hadnt-noticed/","Cisco Talos",{"_114":115,"_116":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
